  "worked_solution": "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(3t)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061t)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e83\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3t),\\,\\frac{1}{t}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3t)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-9\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(3t),\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
